      <statement><p>Let <m>f(x) = 3  -    3 \sqrt{x}</m>.  Then the expression
<me>\frac{f(x + h) - f(x)}{h}</me>
can be written in the form
<me>\frac{A}{\left(\sqrt{Bx + Ch} \right) + \left(\sqrt{x} \right)},</me>
where <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, and <m>C</m> are
constants.  (Note: It's possible for one or more of these
constants to be 0.)  Find the constants.</p>
<p><m>A =</m> <fillin characters="10" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p><m>B =</m> <fillin characters="10" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
<p><m>C =</m> <fillin characters="10" name="AnSwEr0003"/></p>
<p>Use your answer from above to find
<m>\displaystyle{\lim_{h\rightarrow 0} \frac{f(x + h) -
f(x)}{h}}</m>.</p>
<p><m>\displaystyle{\lim_{h\rightarrow 0} \frac{f(x + h) -
f(x)}{h}} =</m> <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0004"/></p>
<p>Finally, find each of the following:</p>
<p><m>f'(1) =</m> <fillin characters="10" name="AnSwEr0005"/></p>
<p><m>f'(2) =</m> <fillin characters="10" name="AnSwEr0006"/></p>
<p><m>f'(3) =</m> <fillin characters="10" name="AnSwEr0007"/></p>
</statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>-3</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>1</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>1</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>\frac{-3}{2\sqrt{x}}</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>\frac{-3}{2\sqrt{1}}</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>\frac{-3}{2\sqrt{2}}</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>\frac{-3}{2\sqrt{3}}</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
      <solution><p>We simplify the expression <me>\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h} = \frac{3-3\sqrt{x+h}-(3-3\sqrt{x})}{h}</me> by multiplying both numerator and denominator by the conjugate and simplifying.</p>
<p><me>\frac{3-3\sqrt{x+h}-(3-3\sqrt{x})}{h} = \frac{-3\sqrt{x+h} + 3\sqrt{x}}{h} =\frac{(-3\sqrt{x+h} + 3\sqrt{x})(-3\sqrt{x+h} - 3\sqrt{x})}{h(-3\sqrt{x+h} - 3\sqrt{x})}</me> 
<me>= \frac{-9\mathopen{}\left(x+h\right) + 9x}{h(-3\sqrt{x+h} - 3\sqrt{x})} = \frac{9h}{h(-3\sqrt{x+h} - 3\sqrt{x})} = \frac{9h}{-3 h(\sqrt{x+h}+\sqrt{x})}</me> 
Finally, we can divide to get
<me>=\frac{-3}{\sqrt{x+h}+\sqrt{x}}</me>
That means A=-3, while B=C=1.</p>
<p>Now, we can take <me>\lim_{h\to 0} \frac{-3}{\sqrt{x+h}+\sqrt{x}} = \frac{-3}{2\sqrt{x}} .</me></p>
<p>Lastly, we can use this to calculate:
<me>f'(1)= \frac{-3}{2\sqrt{1}}</me>
<me>f'(2)= \frac{-3}{2\sqrt{2}}</me>
<me>f'(3)= \frac{-3}{2\sqrt{3}}</me></p>
</solution>
